<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The door routes 1 and 2 knock on. Stage 6, and the answer to
    docs/work-notes/plans/plugin-extension/03-open-questions.md Q-PE-16.

    WHY THIS EXISTS AT ALL. S7 says three front doors and one engine.
    Heron.Installer.App is door three - a WinExe a person double-clicks.
    Routes 1 and 2 are the AI installing, and until this project there was
    nothing for the AI to call: a window needs a hand, a mouse and eyes.
    The gate on route 1 was built and proven with nothing on the other side
    of it.

    WHY NOT ONE OF THE OTHER THREE OPTIONS Q-PE-16 listed.
      - An MCP tool: mcp/ rule 1 is "transport only. No BIM logic, no
        knowledge, no decisions", and it is Python while the engine is C#, so
        it would shell out to something like this anyway.
      - Driving HeronInstaller.exe headless: it is a WinExe, so it either
        draws a window nobody asked for or needs a mode that makes it not a
        window.
      - Driving tools\deploy-addin.ps1 directly: Stage 6's opening line calls
        that FAILURE - "if this stage ends up re-implementing any install
        rule, it has failed" - because it skips InstallPlan and InstallEngine
        entirely. It works today, which is what makes it the easy mistake.

    NO DECIDING IN HERE, for the same reason the window holds none. What may
    be installed is InstallerScreen's answer, what to install is InstallPlan's
    and InstallEngine's, and whether a named source may be used at all is
    InstallSource's. A rule that appears in this project is a rule outside
    everything that tests it.

    net8.0, NOT net8.0-windows, and NOT referencing Heron.Core - the same two
    rules the engine beside it keeps, and for the same reasons. It has no
    window, so it is runnable and testable on the machine this is developed
    on; and Heron.Core follows the Revit release, so referencing it would pin
    a release-independent thing to whichever release happened to be building.

    NOT a Revit add-in. It never loads into Revit.exe.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>Heron.Installer.Cli</RootNamespace>

    <!--
      heron-install, lower case and hyphenated, and the name is a safety
      decision rather than a style one.

      The window beside this is HeronInstaller.exe, and its own .csproj says
      why: "two files a dot apart is a thing somebody eventually double-clicks
      the wrong one of". HeronInstall.exe next to HeronInstaller.exe would be
      two letters apart, which is worse - and one of them draws a window while
      the other does the whole install with no confirmation to read.

      So this one is shaped like a command instead of like an application. It
      is never the file in a Downloads folder; it is the thing the AI calls,
      and it reads as such at a glance.
    -->
    <AssemblyName>heron-install</AssemblyName>
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>

    <!--
      Release-independent, like the engine and the window: it installs FOR a
      Revit release without ever loading into one, so it is built once rather
      than eight times. The list that decides this is in Directory.Build.props,
      because a .csproj is read too late to be seen.
    -->
    <HeronTfm>net8.0</HeronTfm>

    <!--
      A .NET 8 executable on a machine carrying only a later runtime would
      otherwise refuse to start, naming a framework the user has never heard
      of. Same flag, same reason, as Heron.Installer.App and ApiSurface.
    -->
    <RollForward>LatestMajor</RollForward>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Heron.Installer\Heron.Installer.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
